//Types of Modifiers :
//private 
it can only be acess within the class they are defined
//protected
it can be acess within the class and its subclasses
//public    
it can be acess from anywhere
//default
it can be acess within the same package
it is the default modifier if no access modifier is specified
//class
a class can be declared as public or default
it represent the set of  properties and methods that are common to all objects of one type
a classs is a blueprint of an object
//object
an object can be declared as public or default 
//interface
an interface can be declared as public or default
//method
a method can be declared as public, protected, private or default
//variable
a variable can be declared as public, protected, private or default
//constructor
a constructor can be declared as public, protected, private or default  
//polymorphism
polymorphism means the ability to take many forms
it is the ability of a method to do different things based on the object that it is acting upon
//inheritance
inheritance is a mechanism where a new class is derived from an existing class
//method overloading
method overloading is a feature that allows a class to have more than one method with the same name, but different parameters
//method overriding     
method overriding is a feature that allows a subclass to provide a specific implementation of a method that is already defined in its superclass
//dynamic method dispatch
dynamic method dispatch is a mechanism that allows a call to an overridden method to be resolved at runtime
//regular method
a regular method is a method that is defined in a class and can be called on an object
//concrete method
a concrete method is a method that has a body and can be executed
//types ofpolymorphism
//1)compile time polymorphism or static polymorphism or also known as method overloading
it is achieved through method overloading and operator overloading.
this happens when the same method in the sanme class having the same name but different parameters
//2)run time polymorphism also known as dynamic method dispatch
it is achieved through method overriding
it is a process in which a function call to an overridden method and you can aso achieve this only through inheritance

//Data hiding 
it is a mechanism of restricting access to some of the object's components
//data binding(encapsulation)
it is a process that binds the data members and member functions together
//it allows us to focus of what an object does rather than how does
AN abstract class is a class that we can not instatiate and can only extend by subclasses
Abstract class cna have both abstract and non abstract methods
An abstract method is a method that is declared without an implementation
An abstract method must be implemented by the subclass of the abstract class
Ex/ abstract class Animal{
    abstract void sound();
    void sleep(){
        System.out.println("Zzz");
    }
}   
//an abstract classs ony can have only abstract methods and it does not have a body
//an abstract class can not be final because we can not extend a final class
//an interface is a blueprint of a class.it contains only abstract methods and constants
//a class must implement an interface to provide the implementation of the methods declared in the interface
default or static methods 
